WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON: It's
a great question today

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.780
that we ponder.

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:04.830
Was it worth it?

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:08.253
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:18.300
There's a couple of things
to remember about air power

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:19.710
before we begin in earnest.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:22.950
One is that there was an
enormous transformation

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:28.200
between its inaugural appearance
in 1914 in World War I

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:33.600
when pilots and biplanes
shot revolvers at each other.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:38.040
In less than 30 years--
about 25 years, 24 years--

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:42.870
at the outbreak in September
1, 1939 in World War II,

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:45.030
we had monoplanes,
and the speeds

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:49.140
had increased from 100
miles an hour to nearly 300.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:52.800
And people were firing
about 700 to 800 shots

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.440
per minute on machine
guns, sometimes

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.900
right through the propeller,
sometimes on the wings.

00:00:57.900 --> 00:00:59.700
And they were
carrying bomb loads

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.850
in the case of fire bombers
of 500 to 1,000 pounds

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:05.019
and bombers even far greater.

00:01:05.019 --> 00:01:08.070
So there was a
miraculous transformation

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:09.690
in the role of air power.

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:12.700
And that created our
architects of air power,

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:14.580
so to speak, in the
European countries

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.230
and in the United
States as well--

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:19.620
Billy Mitchell, Douhet,
Weaver in Germany.

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:22.800
And they were so enthralled
by this new weapon

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:26.580
that they made a series
of astounding predictions,

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:28.740
so much that Prime
Minister Stanley

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:32.370
Baldwin in the late 1930s said
in despair, the bomber will

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:33.240
always get through.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:39.990
And it was almost as if assets
at sea, navies, and assets

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:42.630
on the ground, infantry,
were going to be

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:44.580
secondary or even irrelevant.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:47.470
The war would take
place up in the air.

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:49.800
The problem with that is
nobody lives up in the air.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:50.850
Nobody eats in the air.

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:52.620
Nobody can fly.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:53.680
We don't have wings.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:57.270
So what you're doing is
transferring labor and capital

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:59.640
from the ground up into the air.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:01.950
And there has to be
a purpose for that.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:05.910
And the purpose had to be
either air superiority or air

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:07.080
supremacy.

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:09.720
And by that I mean,
your air forces

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:13.020
had to beat the other person's
air forces for the prime reason

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.600
that they could then direct
their assets to where things

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.650
really mattered on the ground--

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:20.670
taking cities or territory
or killing people

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:22.650
or capturing them on the ground.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:26.590
If you couldn't do that, and
you only achieved air parity,

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:28.770
then both sides were sort
of wasting their time

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:30.720
in an irrelevant theater.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:34.590
So the purpose was to build
more planes and better planes

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:38.700
and train better pilots to
destroy the enemy's air assets

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.940
or at least weaken them so
you had air superiority.

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:45.330
And then, the holy
grail was air supremacy.

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:48.510
That meant that you could put
your assets up in the air,

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:52.560
they could fly over the enemy's
territory, destroy his cities

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.440
or his armies on the ground,
and they would be invulnerable

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.620
because you had destroyed
the enemy's ability

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:00.630
to fight back in the air.

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:02.610
And that was pretty
much the theory

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:05.040
of air power in World War II.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:10.170
It's a great question today
that we ponder, was it worth it?

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:11.670
The people say it didn't work.

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:13.650
80,000 people weren't worth it.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:16.530
We lost over 5,000
bomber planes.

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:19.170
We spent 40% of our
military budget.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:23.550
And yet, German industrial
production kept going.

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:25.350
The defenders of air
power said, well,

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:28.110
we had to make the
necessary investments.

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:30.660
We lost men and
material learning

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.330
how to fly in the
proper formation,

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:36.180
how to have the proper
fighter, the proper fighter

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:37.920
tactics, the proper radar.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.000
But once we learned that,
we destroyed Germany's

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.110
industrial production.

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:45.090
And it's not quite
accurate to say

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:49.650
it continued to increase in
terms of vehicle or aircraft

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:52.470
production because Germany
really wasn't mobilized

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:56.610
until Albert Speer, the
tsar of German industry,

00:03:56.610 --> 00:04:00.120
took control over the
economy in late 1943.

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:03.570
Once he did that,
we were destroying

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:05.520
the increase in production.

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:09.610
And Speer's planned economy
was not sustainable.

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:12.930
So even though it increased,
it did not increase at the rate

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:15.870
that it would otherwise
have had we not been

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:17.640
bombing around the clock.

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:20.790
The United States lost
about 400,000 dead

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:23.310
through accidents,
disease, and combat.

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:26.700
Britain lost a little bit
fewer and yet defeated

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:29.430
a far more formidable
enemy than was

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:33.060
true in World War
I. In other words,

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:38.760
Germany, Japan, and Italy
were far more powerful allies

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:42.300
and enemies than had
been the Imperial German

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:43.350
Army of World War I.

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:44.500
And how did they do that?

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:47.400
And how did they lose
only about 2% to 3%

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:49.410
of their entire military forces?

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:54.210
And the answer was sea power
and air power did one thing--

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:59.430
they avoided the need
to land in Europe

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:04.200
at the very beginning of 1940,
'41 in the case of British

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:07.680
and the United States
in '42 or '43 in Japan.

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:09.390
In other words,
we did not do what

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:13.410
we did in 1917 in
the case of America

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:14.990
and the British in 1914.

00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:18.050
We did not bleed white
our ground forces

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:21.710
to take the enemy capital and
knock the enemy out of the war.

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:25.400
We bombed them into submission
and therefore precluded

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:29.180
an invasion of Japan entirely.

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:31.460
And we precluded an
invasion of Europe

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:34.340
until we were ready
to do it in June 1944.

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:37.410
So the ultimate verdict,
I think, on air power

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:40.400
was it was very
costly, it did not

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:44.180
work too well in
'41, '42, and '43,

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:46.970
but the Allies
gained the lessons

00:05:46.970 --> 00:05:51.590
and achieved air superiority and
eventually air supremacy that

00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:56.490
by 1944, 1945, it saved
thousands of lives.

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:58.910
And it was one of the greatest
successes of World War II.

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:05.290
